  "answer_text": "Great question! Here's a detailed breakdown of why men tend to have larger Adam's apples:\n\n---\n\n### What Is the Adam's Apple?\n\nThe Adam's apple is a prominence of the thyroid cartilage that partially encloses the larynx (the voice box). Adam's apples are found on both women and men \u2014 they just show up more prominently in men. Also known as the laryngeal prominence, the Adam's apple sits right on top of the thyroid gland, so the area is fittingly called the thyroid cartilage.\n\n---\n\n### Why Is It Larger in Men?\n\nThe main reason comes down to **puberty and testosterone**:\n\n1. **Equal at birth:** Girls and boys start out with similarly sized thyroid cartilage, but this changes when they hit puberty.\n\n2. **Testosterone drives growth:** In men, the production of testosterone causes the larynx to grow larger, resulting in a deeper voice and a more prominent Adam's apple.\n\n3. **More growth in males:** The reason it's more prominent in men than in women is because during puberty, a man's voice box tends to grow in size (much more than in women), so a noticeable bump appears right in the middle of the throat.\n\n4. **Shape difference:** There is also a difference in the shape of the laryngeal prominence in males and females. In males, the sides of the laryngeal prominence protrude at an almost 90\u00b0 angle, while in females, it is at a more obtuse 120\u00b0 angle, making it appear less visible.\n\n---\n\n### Why Does It Matter for Voice?\n\nOnce puberty starts, hormones (especially testosterone) cause the vocal cords to grow bigger in males. The thyroid cartilage therefore sticks out a bit more to protect these larger vocal cords. People with a larger Adam's apple tend to have a deeper voice than people with a smaller one.\n\n---\n\n### Do Women Have Adam's Apples?\n\nYes, women do have Adam's apples, but they are usually much less prominent than in men. Both men and women have the same structures in their throats, including the thyroid cartilage, but in women, it tends to be smaller and less noticeable. Women who have higher levels of testosterone, either naturally or due to medical conditions, may experience more masculine traits, including a larger larynx and a more noticeable Adam's apple.\n\n---\n\n### An Evolutionary Angle\n\nResearchers have proposed that the larynx's growth allowed individuals to produce vocalizations that mimic a larger body size. In humans, a deeper, more resonant voice may have helped adult males sound more imposing relative to females and younger males, giving them an edge in social competition.\n\n---\n\nIn short, it all comes down to testosterone during puberty causing the male larynx to grow significantly larger, pushing the thyroid cartilage outward and creating that visible bump on the throat.",
